1.2Vision Statement
VISION
To build the first football intelligence system that reasons in causes rather than
correlations — one that can watch a match the way a world-class analyst watches
it, but explain what it sees the way a scientist explains a result: with a hypothesis,
an intervention, and evidence for what would have happened otherwise.
Every component described in this document — from ByteTrack-based player tracking to a
Neo4j knowledge graph to a retrieval-grounded football LLM — exists in service of that
one sentence. Nothing in the stack is there for its own sake; each layer either produces the
causal evidence or communicates it.
1.3Philosophy
Before any code is written, FCIE requires a shift in how football is conceptualised. The
temptation with any sports-AI project is to start from the metrics everyone already knows
— expected goals, possession share, pass completion — and try to make them smarter . FCIE
deliberately starts somewhere else: from six adjacent fields that already know how to reas‐
on about systems that unfold in time, under uncertainty, with many interacting agents.
