## Page 30

⑧ Causal Reasoning Engine REASONING RESEARCH CORE
PURPOSE Identify Domino Moments and rank them by estimated causal influence. The heart of
FCIE — full theory in Part VI.
RESPONSIBILITIES Candidate decision-point search over the Decision Chain (Section 2.12); influence es‐
timation; importance ranking; evidence packaging for downstream explanation.
INPUTS St, τt, Decision Chain graph; counterfactual estimates from Module ⑨.
OUTPUTS Ranked Domino Moment list with influence scores and supporting evidence, written
to Module ⑦.
INTERNAL AL‐
GORITHMS
Attention attribution; Shapley-style causal credit assignment; graph influence
propagation; temporal Granger-inspired discovery (Part VI, §6.4).
DEPENDENCIES Module ⑨ for counterfactual queries; Module ⑥ for long-range context.
FAILURE MODES Confounded influence estimates when opponent quality or game-state is not ad‐
equately controlled for; small per-match sample sizes limiting statistical confidence.
SCALABILITY Post-match batch mode is embarrassingly parallel across matches; live in-match
ranking requires a lower-latency approximate mode.
FUTURE IM‐
PROVEMENTS
Cross-match causal-effect pooling to shrink small-sample estimates toward league-
level priors.
PART IV — SOFTWARE ARCHITECTURE FOOTBALL CAUSAL INTELLIGENCE ENGINE
30

## Page 31

⑨ Counterfactual Simulator REASONING
PURPOSE Answer "what happens next if this action had been different" — full framework in
Part VII.
RESPONSIBILITIES Perturb St at a candidate decision point; roll forward under a learned world model
with opponent-adaptation; compare against the observed continuation.
INPUTS Candidate decision point from Module ⑧; world-model weights (Part V, §5.7).
OUTPUTS Counterfactual trajectory samples; influence-score components consumed by Module
⑧.
INTERNAL AL‐
GORITHMS
Learned world-model rollout; synthetic-control matching against similar historical
situations; generative trajectory sampling.
DEPENDENCIES GPU inference (Module ⑯); historical St corpus for matching-based methods.
FAILURE MODES Sim-to-real gap between learned rollouts and true human behaviour under novel
perturbations (Part XIV, §14.5); compounding error over long rollout horizons.
SCALABILITY Rollouts are independently parallelisable across candidate decision points and
sample counts.
FUTURE IM‐
PROVEMENTS
Multi-agent RL-trained opponent policies in place of the current heuristic adaptation
model (Part V, §5.8).
PART IV — SOFTWARE ARCHITECTURE FOOTBALL CAUSAL INTELLIGENCE ENGINE
