## Page 69

N
othing in this Part should be read as a claim that commercialisation is
the point of FCIE — Parts I through XII stand on their own as a research
programme. This Part exists because the original brief asked what happens if
the research does leave the lab, and because thinking honestly about a market
forces useful discipline back onto the technical roadmap.
13.1Business Model
A tiered B2B SaaS model fits the buyer landscape better than a single price point. A youth
academy tier offers the simplified, development-focused dashboard from Part X at low
cost, prioritising adoption and data volume over margin. A professional club tier offers
the full Coach AI, analyst tooling, and scouting/recruitment modules, priced against the
analyst headcount it augments rather than replaces. A broadcaster / media tier licenses
Match Narrative generation (§2.14) for enhanced post-match content, a genuinely different
buyer with a different willingness to pay and a much higher volume, lower-margin-per-
match economics. A separate, smaller research licensing track — the Knowledge Graph
schema and benchmark dataset from Part XII, item 21 — is offered to academic groups at
low or no cost, both as a genuine public contribution and as a credibility and recruiting
channel.
13.2Customers & Market
The buyer set spans professional clubs (first-team and academy analytics departments),
federations (national-team and youth-pathway analysis), and media/broadcast organisa‐
tions. Betting and wagering-adjacent analytics firms are a real, distinct segment of the ex‐
isting sports-data market — mentioned here only as a factual feature of the competitive
landscape, not a direction FCIE's own roadmap targets, since the product's value proposi‐
tion (explainable, coach-facing causal reasoning) is a poor fit for that use case regardless.
13.3Competitive Landscape
The market is not empty, and pretending otherwise would undercut the credibility of
everything  else  in  this  document.  Established  sports-data  and  analytics  providers  —
among them StatsBomb, Opta/Stats Perform, SkillCorner , and Second Spectrum — already
supply high-quality event and tracking data, and several offer their own valuation and
visualisation layers on top of it. FCIE's differentiated position is not "better tracking data"
— it does not need to compete on that axis at all in an early stage, since it can ingest data
from these same providers — but the causal reasoning and explanation layer sitting above
PART XIII — STARTUP VISION FOOTBALL CAUSAL INTELLIGENCE ENGINE
69

## Page 70

the data, which is not, to current public knowledge, a mature offering from any of them.
That is a genuine gap, but it is also the kind of gap an incumbent with existing club rela‐
tionships and tracking infrastructure could close quickly once the value is proven, which is
the central strategic risk in §13.4.
13.4Risks
Data and rights access. Broadcast video rights and league-level tracking data access
are gated and often exclusive; a commercial version of FCIE needs data-partnership
agreements that a research prototype can sidestep.
Incumbent response. Existing providers with deeper club relationships and existing
tracking  pipelines  are  well  positioned  to  build  a  competing  causal  layer  once  FCIE
demonstrates the category is valuable — the classic fast-follower risk for any single-
layer innovation on top of someone else's platform.
GPU and inference cost at the scale of full-league, near-real-time deployment, particu‐
larly for the Counterfactual Simulator's rollout-heavy workload (Part IV, §4.5, Module
⑯).
Trust and adoption. Coaching staffs are (rightly) sceptical of black-box recommenda‐
tions; adoption depends entirely on the explainability discipline in Part IX, §9.3 actually
holding up under real scrutiny, not just performing well in a demo.
Causal validity risk. Part VI and Part XIV are explicit that causal claims from observa‐
tional  sports  data  carry  real,  unresolved  uncertainty  —  overselling  confidence  to  a
paying customer is both a commercial and an intellectual-integrity risk.
13.5Funding Strategy
A milestone-based approach follows naturally from the roadmap in Part XI rather than a
generic fundraising narrative: pre-seed or grant funding covering Years 1–2 (tracking and
state-representation foundations, where the deliverable is research credibility, not reven‐
ue); a seed round timed to Year 3's Domino Moment and counterfactual research results,
which are the first outputs a technical investor could actually evaluate; and a Series A
timed to Year 4's pilot club deployment, once there is a real usage signal rather than a re‐
search claim to underwrite the raise. Grant and academic-partnership funding (university
co-supervision, national research-council programmes for applied AI) is a plausible, cur‐
rently under-used channel for Years 1–2 specifically, given how much of that period is
genuine open research rather than product engineering.
