15.9Further Reading
This document proposes original formalism throughout Part II, but it does not pretend to
do so in a vacuum. Readers who want the intellectual lineage behind specific pieces should
look  to:  Judea  Pearl's  work  on  structural  causal  models  and  do-calculus  (accessibly
summarised in  The Book of Why, written with Dana Mackenzie) for everything in §2.13
and Part VI–VII; the probabilistic pitch-control research associated with Will Spearman for
§2.7; Karun Singh's Expected Threat framework and Tom Decroos and colleagues' VAEP
work (KU Leuven) as the possession-valuation lineage discussed critically in §1.5 and Part
III; and the broader graph neural network and temporal-graph-learning literature for the
modelling choices in §5.3 and Part VIII. Open tracking datasets released by providers such
as Metrica Sports are a practical starting point for Year 1 of the roadmap (Part XI) before
any club data-sharing agreement is in place.
